      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T11:42:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e It should probably be safe even on the first cpu as well - meaning we could set the static key after deprivilege is successful?\n\nMy bad, `create_iommu` needs to be called before enabling the hypercalls. So yes, we need the first cpu to to complete init_finalize before enabling the static key.",
      "parentUuid": "c99ba14d_92802dcc",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "abd03859_6ed55745",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T15:11:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e To prevent that, I suppose we should set the pkvm_enabled_key after the first finalise, not all the finalise.\n\nThen we have a small window when pkvm_enabled_key is already set but a CPU has not finalised yet, but is already trying to use the hypercall.\n\nAlthough I see you\u0027ve already said about that:\n\n\u003e ...the other CPUs would use hypercall earlier before its finalise, but sounds like should not be a problem as the pkvm hypervisor is already able to handle this, right?\n\nWell, yes, it seems it would be already able to handle this hypercall.\n\nBut it means introducing another non-obvious assumption... And I imagined the meaning of this global pkvm_enabled flag is that pKVM is fully initialized (and we can reuse this flag in KVM MMU and so on...). Well, yes, maybe it\u0027s not a bad idea to set it to true already after the first finalise, and reset it back to false if something fails later, and add a comment explaining why we do it that way... (Even though it might not address any real issue as of now, assuming that in fact IRQ_REMAP never runs in parallel with pKVM deprivileging, so there is no real issue.)\n\n...Regarding instruction decoder, IMHO we should try to avoid the need for such complicated error-prone stuff in the hypervisor.",
      "parentUuid": "777ca606_647a9397",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d552ec09_6981a96f",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-26T02:47:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e and reset it back to false if something fails later\n\nI didn\u0027t realized about resetting it back to false for recovery. If any of the finialise hypercall is failed, we goes to reprivilege. As the pkvm_enabled_key is a global, when shall we reset it back to false if it is set to be true after the first success finialise hypercall? If we reset it back to false after all cpu reprivileged, then some already reprivileged CPU can still use hypercall to access IOMMU MMIO (even though it might not be happened for the IRQ_REMAP driver) and cause #UD in that CPU. If we reset it back before reprivilege any CPU, then it can cause EPT violation. Seems no good way to reset back the pkvm_enabled_key, unless we switch to use percpu variable?",
      "parentUuid": "abd03859_6ed55745",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "26543762_c4970ef8",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-26T14:17:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, you are right, it is tricky. It\u0027s better not to rely on pkvm_enabled_key  while deprivileging is still in progress. (Although pkvm_enabled_key might be still useful to have, for various checks, not during deprivileging but later, after everything is deprivileged and finalized.)\n\nSo I think we have 2 options:\n\n* If we still think that IOMMU MMIO access (by IRQ_REMAP or anything else) may possibly happen during deprivileging/finalizing, we should just use percpu variables, like we used to. Maybe rename them to pkvm_cpu_enabled (to not conflict with the global pkvm_enabled()), and use them in dmar_XXX() accessors, while in other places use the global pkvm_enabled().\n\n* Or if we think that IOMMU MMIO access may not happen during deprivileging/finalizing, then we can just have pkvm_enabled_key, without percpu variables, to keep the code simpler.",
      "parentUuid": "d552ec09_6981a96f",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "14fefd74_92b3790a",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-26T15:07:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was again thinking along the lines of cpu synchronization and I feel the basic problem here is that cpus are not in a controlled state of execution while being initialized for pkvm. If we can make sure that all cpus entered into a controlled and predictable sequence of execution, we can guarantee that, iommu MMIO will not happen. The sequence I am envisioning is:\n1. main cpu enter vmx_pkvm_init and performs deprivilege\n2. Sets up a shared area to communicate the state of the INIT_FINALIZE phase\n2. Does the INIT_FINALIZE hypercall\n3. sends ipi to other cpus to do INIT_FINALIZE hypercall, but does not wait(sync \u003d false)\n4. All the other cpus perform the hypercall, but waits before returning. It updates the shared area about its status of the init_finalize\n5. Main cpu waits for other cpus to update the status. It reads the status and if all the cpus succeeds, it enables the static key\n6. Main cpu updates the shared area about the final status\n7. Other cpus wait for this update and it returns from the hypercall after reading the status. if global status is failure, it reprivileges\n\nA sample code is here:\nhttps://gist.github.com/vineethrp/ed0dab4ab93ec14eb72135daf5774ef5\n\nThis code is just compile tested. Need to test and may need changes to make it functional. Similar idea to completion variables, but doing it with bare primitives. Just a PoC that I wanted to share it with you all and see how it looks like :-)\n\nI think its not super complex and is good to have this logic in here so that we have a controlled state of execution during deprivilege and initialization. What do you all think about this?",
      "parentUuid": "26543762_c4970ef8",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d1a6f61d_dd1895e8",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-27T09:29:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003eSo I think we have 2 options:\n\nI am fine with either case. Just a little worry about if we can convince upstream with option#2 in the future :). No concern for now.\n\n\u003eI think its not super complex and is good to have this logic in here so that we have a controlled state of execution during deprivilege and initialization. What do you all think about this?\n\nSo we will block all the CPU a while until all the CPU successfully finalised or reprivileged. I agree this is a good idea to solve the issues with just using one global pkvm_enabled_key.\n\nThis reminds me that, with single global pkvm_enabled_key, we are fine to set this to true after the first finalise if all the other CPUs can also be finalised successfully. The only issue is for reprivilege when any of the CPU is finalised failed. I think the reason is that, the deprivilege and finalise are two separate phases, while the reprivilege is one combined phase. In deprivilege, we just put the host in non-root mode while doesn\u0027t use EPT for the host. So before a CPU calling finalise to switch to EPT, this CPU is able to use hypercall already. The reprivilege is different, it disable VMX directly to let the host back to root mode directly. If we can split the reprivilege to two phases, e.g., use the reprivilege hypercall to disable EPT only. Then the pkvm_enabled_key can be reset back to false after disabling EPT for all the CPU. After that, disable VMX for all the CPU.",
      "parentUuid": "14fefd74_92b3790a",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
